1. Overall Inventory Sufficiency

Determine the percentage of items that are above, near, or below their Reorder Point (ROP) and Safety Stock.

Highlight which categories, brands, or subgroups have the highest risk.

Provide a summary sentence such as: “X% of items are at risk, mostly in the [Subgroup/Brand].”

2. Replenishment Needs

Summarize the total replenishment quantity required.

Highlight items with the highest urgency (top 5) including ITEM_DESC, CLO_BALANCE_QTY, Reorder_Point, and Replenishment_Qty.

Mention any items that are moderate risk and should be monitored in the next purchase cycle.

3. Shortage Risks

Quantify how many items are in the “At Risk” and “Moderate” categories.

Provide examples of critical shortages with values: e.g., “Item A (Closing 20, Safety Stock 50 → Shortage 30).”

Calculate total shortage quantity and approximate value.

4. Trends & Patterns

Identify patterns across item groups, subgroups, or brands.

Highlight which categories or subgroups are consistently overstocked or understocked.

Mention if certain brands or classes are more prone to shortages.

5. Recommendations

For “At Risk” items → immediate replenishment required.

For “Moderate” items → monitor and plan for next procurement cycle.

For overstocked items → slow down procurement to avoid excess stock.

Suggest adjustments to Safety Stock or lead times if shortages are frequent.

6. Examples

Provide 2–3 concrete examples per section for clarity.

Include ITEM_DESC, ITEM_CODE, current stock, required quantity, and shortage value where relevant.

7. Formatting & Delivery

Organize insights in clear sections with bullet points.

Provide numeric values, percentages, and actionable recommendations.

Make insights dashboard-ready so they can be used in reports or presentations.